  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c18dce35c4f218942974316577a84ef5, type: 3}
  m_Name: Scene05_Context
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::OhmsLaw.SceneAgentContext
  sceneId: Scene05
  sceneTitle: Journey Inside the Wire
  askWattsonEnabled: 1
  covered:
  - 'Everything from Scene 4: the Christmas-lights mystery, Georg Ohm, the words
    voltage and current, the Ohm-niscope.'
  - Inside a metal the charge carriers are free electrons.
  - Before a voltage is applied the electrons move randomly in every direction with
    no net drift.
  - A voltage is a potential difference between two points; applying it across the
    wire sets up an electric field that pushes the electrons. Symbol V, unit volt
    (V).
  - "With the voltage applied the electrons keep their random motion and all drift
    together, from the \u2212 terminal end toward the + terminal end. Conventional
    current is drawn the other way, + to \u2212."
  - "Current is the charge crossing a section of the wire each second (I = \u0394Q/\u0394t).
    Symbol I, unit ampere (A). The counter in the panel counts a few representative
    charges per second; a real wire carries billions."
  - The scene shows only a small, evenly spread sample of the free electrons so one
    can be followed; the drift shown is a picture, real drift is very slow.
  doNotSpoil:
  - How V and I are related
  - V is proportional to I
  - V = IR
  - R = V / I
  - Resistance
  - Ohms
  - The name 'Ohm's law'
  - The answer to the Christmas-lights mystery
  faq: {fileID: 11400000, guid: e545d3c62bba2464d923592aa07c0cce, type: 2}
